
This directory is intended for project header files.

Project responsibilities:
- IO exposes raw input states in OFF, HALF, FULL, AUX order (LOW is active).
- Control owns input priority (FULL, HALF, OFF), operating levels, the three-hour
  timeout and countdown, and flap commands. The RGB LED is not used: the
  NINA module that drives it runs Bluetooth.
  The countdown runs only in HALF/FULL and restarts when switching between them.
  On expiry, TIMEOUT stays latched until a different level is selected.
  No active input retains the last selection; inactive levels show zero time left.
- Oled reads the timeout countdown from Control.
- Bluetooth (bluetooth.h) runs BLE from boot and advertises the identification
  name. OtaCore (ota_core.h) is the transport-independent update protocol,
  fw_marker.h embeds the firmware version, IdentityCore (identity_core.h)
  stores the name in /ota/NAME.TXT on the SFU flash filesystem, and
  Diagnostics (diagnostics.h) is the snapshot published every 500 ms.

Bluetooth firmware update:
1. Run `uv run --project tools ble-flash --generate-secret` once. This writes the
   git-ignored include/ota_secret.h, which the firmware and the script share.
   Without it, the firmware does not build.
2. Flash once over USB. The image includes the Arduino SFU bootloader in its
   first 64 KB, so the application starts at 0x10010000. BLE needs NINA
   firmware 3.0.0 or newer.
3. Bump PARAMS_VERSION_* in params.h and build. Only newer versions are
   accepted.
4. Bluetooth is always on; the OLED "BLE" page shows the connection and update
   progress (press 0 there to retry if it shows "BLE start fail"). Cab control
   keeps running during the update.
5. Run `uv run --project tools ble-flash [--name "Cab 3"]`, or connect in the
   TUI (below) and press u. tools/ is a uv project (pyproject.toml, uv.lock);
   uv creates and syncs tools/.venv on first run.
The image is authenticated with HMAC-SHA256 (shared secret plus a per-attempt
nonce) and verified (SHA-256, SFU present, version marker) before it is
handed to SFU. SFU installs it on the next boot. There is no automatic
rollback: if the new firmware misbehaves, double-tap reset and flash over USB.

Bluetooth diagnostics, identification name and firmware update (TUI):
`uv run --project tools cab-tui` lists nearby cabinets with their name and
firmware version (advertised by the device; "?" for firmware that does not
advertise it yet); Enter connects and shows live power, control, input and
system diagnostics. Press n to set the identification name (1-15 printable
ASCII characters). Press u to update the firmware: the dialog defaults to the PlatformIO firmware.bin (or --firmware),
shows its version next to the device's and only accepts newer images; Escape
aborts a running upload. Setting the name and updating need the same
include/ota_secret.h; reading diagnostics does not. The name
is kept across reboots and firmware updates, shown on the welcome screen and
the OLED "Name" page, and advertised over BLE once the TUI disconnects.

A header file is a file containing C declarations and macro definitions
to be shared between several project source files. You request the use of a
header file in your project source file (C, C++, etc) located in `src` folder
by including it, with the C preprocessing directive `#include'.

```src/main.c

#include "header.h"

int main (void)
{
 ...
}
```

Including a header file produces the same results as copying the header file
into each source file that needs it. Such copying would be time-consuming
and error-prone. With a header file, the related declarations appear
in only one place. If they need to be changed, they can be changed in one
place, and programs that include the header file will automatically use the
new version when next recompiled. The header file eliminates the labor of
finding and changing all the copies as well as the risk that a failure to
find one copy will result in inconsistencies within a program.

In C, the convention is to give header files names that end with `.h'.

Read more about using header files in official GCC documentation:

* Include Syntax
* Include Operation
* Once-Only Headers
* Computed Includes

https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/cpp/Header-Files.html
